              model_answer:
                'Between 1914 and 1918, the First World War devastated the social fabric of rural Great Britain, transforming small, quiet parishes like Stubbington and Hill Head into communities of permanent bereavement [1.1]. The conflict exerted a profound, lasting impact on the village through four primary dimensions: catastrophic demographic loss, the total extinction of prominent family lineages like the Lowrys, the unresolved psychological agony of missing bodies, and the physical immortalization of grief in civic memorials [1.1, 2.1, 3.1].\n\nFirst and foremost, the war caused catastrophic demographic depletion in the local parish [1.1]. In 1914, Stubbington and Hill Head were tightly-knit agricultural and coastal communities with a population of barely 1,500 residents. When the call to arms came, hundreds of farm labourers, dockyard workers, and schoolteachers volunteered for the Hampshire Regiment (Pompey Pals) and the Royal Navy [1.1]. By 1918, 67 young men and women from the parish had been killed [1.1]. This loss represented approximately 20% of the village’s young male workforce, permanently depriving local farms, workshops, and businesses of labour, and leaving dozens of young women without marriage partners in an era defined as the "Lost Generation".\n\nSecondly, the tragedy of the war was felt with acute, concentrated horror in individual households, epitomized by the devastating fate of the Lowry family of Manor Way Grange [2.1]. William and Annie Lowry sent all three of their beloved sons to the front, and in an agonizing tragedy, not one returned [2.1]. William "Harper" Lowry (25) was killed leading a charge up a ravine at Gallipoli in 1915; Cyril "Patrick" Lowry (20) was killed in action during the 1918 German Spring Offensive in full view of his commanding older brother; and Major Auriol "Eric" Lowry DSO, MC (25) was killed by a machine-gun bullet just seven weeks before the Armistice [2.1, 2.2]. The complete extinction of an entire family line demonstrated the democratic, indiscriminate brutality of industrial warfare, which destroyed wealthy landed families just as ruthlessly as working-class homes [2.3].\n\nFurthermore, the impact was intensified by the psychological agony of the "missing" [1.2, 4.2]. Because modern artillery shells dismembered bodies or buried them in churned mud, the corpses of William Lowry, Patrick Lowry, and dozens of other Stubbington soldiers were never recovered for Christian burial [2.1, 4.2]. For grieving parents, having no grave to visit in France or Turkey caused unresolved, perpetual grief. Consequently, local war memorials took on an intensely sacred, surrogate function [4.3]. When the village erected the unique oak shelter over the old village pump on Stubbington Green in 1922, it became a local tomb [1.1]. Local carpenter Arthur Tribbeck built the shelter with his own hands, enduring the unbearable sorrow of carving the wooden beam that held the name of his own 21-year-old son, Harold Tribbeck, who had died of gangrene in 1918 [1.3].\n\nFinally, the memorial preserved the often-forgotten sacrifices of women, exemplified by Nita Madeline King [3.1]. The only woman commemorated on the Stubbington Green memorial, Nita volunteered for Queen Mary\'s Army Auxiliary Corps (QMAAC) and died of cerebrospinal meningitis in 1917 while nursing wounded troops at Wimereux military hospital in France [3.1]. Her devastated mother, Lydia King, donated £200 to fund the memorial shelter, ensuring that female service in military hospitals was recognized alongside combat soldiers [3.1].\n\nIn conclusion, the First World War permanently altered Stubbington [4.1, 4.3]. As cultural historian Jay Winter demonstrates, village memorials were not erected to celebrate imperial militarism, but to serve as essential physical sanctuaries of collective grief [4.3]. The 67 names carved into the oak beams of Stubbington Green stand as an enduring local testament to a shattered generation whose absence echoed through the village for decades.',
